Experiment 1.1 Contain duplicate (leetcode-217)

Aim:-
To write a C++ program that checks whether an integer array contains any duplicate elements. If any element appears more than once, return true; otherwise, return false.
Algorithm (Using Sorting):
1. Start.
2. Sort the given array in ascending order.
3. Traverse the sorted array from index 0 to n-2.
4. Compare each element with its next element.
5. If nums[i] == nums[i+1], return true because a duplicate exists.
6. If no duplicate is found after the loop, return false.
7. Stop.

Code:-
class Solution {

public:

    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(),nums.end());
        int n =nums.size();
        for(int i=0;i<n-1;i++){
            if(nums[i]==nums[i+1]){
                    return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};

Experiment 1.2 Conatain duplicate (leetcode-219)

Aim:-
To write a C++ program that determines whether an integer array contains two equal elements whose indices differ by at most k. Return true if such a pair exists; otherwise, return false.

Algorithm (Using Hash Map):
1. Start.
2. Create an unordered_map to store each element and its latest index.
3. Traverse the array from left to right.
4. For each element:
5. Check if it already exists in the map.
6. If it exists, calculate the index difference:
7. If (current_index - previous_index) <= k, return true.
8. Update the current element's index in the map.
9. If the loop completes without finding a valid pair, return false.
9. Stop.

Code:-
class Solution {

public:

    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_map<int,int>mp;
        int n=nums.size();
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            if(mp.find(nums[i])!=mp.end()){
                if(i-mp[nums[i]]<=k){
                    return true;
                }
            }
            mp[nums[i]]=i;
        }
        return false;
    }
};
